From: StefanVonRanda <16295563+StefanVonRanda@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:02:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] Flatten transparent sources onto grey; --keep-alpha for
 edits

What was missing: load_image and load_reference did convert("RGB"), so a
transparent PNG used as init or reference reached the VAE with whatever
colour sat under alpha 0 (black for most cutouts), and an edit of a cutout
came back opaque.

- pipeline.py: flatten() composites any image with alpha (RGBA, LA, PA,
  palette/RGB with a transparency key) onto neutral grey (128) before
  encoding; load_image and load_reference use it. Request.keep_alpha (CLI
  --keep-alpha): after decode the result's alpha is the source's (picture 1
  of an instruction edit, stretched to the output; else the init image,
  cropped like load_image). With a mask the kept pixels already come from
  the init, so they keep its colours and its alpha. validate() refuses
  keep_alpha without init_image or references.
- Alternative tried once and left out: --rgba (RGBA VAE) + alpha LoRA
  stacked with the edit LoRA on the fox edit below. It recoloured the fox
  but punched holes in the body (alpha > 245 fell from 18.8 % to 9.2 % of
  pixels, the robe semi-transparent); the alpha LoRA is not trained for
  edits.

Checked: pytest 50 passed (new: transparent PNG flattened to 128 in
load_image and load_reference with opaque pixels untouched; source_alpha
for a reference stretched to the output, an init centre-cropped, an opaque
image = 255; keep_alpha without a source refused). Real edit on compact,
8-step DMD + chinoll edit LoRA, CFG 1, 1024^2, fox cutout as picture 1,
"Make the fox's coat bright green, keep everything else", keep_alpha:
RGBA PNG whose alpha equals the source's exactly (mean |diff| 0), fur and
clothes green, 67 s denoise, 11.1 GB MLX peak.
Not checked: keep_alpha with a mask on real weights; edits that move or
add things (the source outline is reused as is, so they get clipped);
semi-transparent edge pixels carry a little grey from the flattening.
